i got into CPU design for mainly two reasons :
  - i'm a HW guy and CPUs are the electronic Graal.
  - I do SW as well and it's painful because other HW designers
     made stupid decisions, making SW development uselessly inefficient.
If that can reassure you, i have slowly and painfully learnt all i know,
i wasn't born knowing and doing all this ;-)
Furthermore, knowledge is not enough : DOING is the real thing.
Any piece of source code that we write counts.
Anything that encourages freedom and well-being (starting
with our computer's design, so we can write good code easily)
is a step forward.

Damnit, just to give a typical example :
Debian 2.6.8 on XP900, a friend just gave
me a LSI 53C1010-based SCSI card.
Great, it's a 64-bit PCI card, it should run fast !
I plug a SCSI backplane with 4x10Krpm 18GB disks.
hdparm -t /dev/sda = 53MB/s (nice)
hdparm -t /dev/sdb = 8MB/s (heck)
hdparm -t /dev/sdc = 8MB/s (what?)
hdparm -t /dev/sdd = 8MB/s (**ck)

This is a SW issue in the kernel (swapping the disks
brings the first slot to highest speed). The reason i was told :
The driver developpers have chosen to limit
the speed of the non-first unit because of eventual stability issues.
I had the same problem with other SCSI cards (reference forgotten).
In fact i have never gotten the full speed on a complete SCSI chain on Alpha :-(
This is the kind of thing that makes me angry and want a better world.

But now i must sleep :-)
